# An `if` that yields one of two functions, neither of which says what
# it is.
#
# Both branches have the same type as far as anything can tell -- "a
# function nobody has worked out yet" -- so taking one to stand for both
# would mean checking `shout`'s body every time the value `whisper`
# returned is called. There are no type variables to say "whichever of
# the two it turns out to be", so the checker says so and asks for an
# annotation instead of quietly checking the wrong function.
#
# This program is expected to FAIL type checking. Its `.ast.out` holds
# the error message, not a program's output.

def shout(word):
    word ++ '!'

def whisper(word):
    word ++ '...'

def chooser(loud):
    if loud:
        shout
    else:
        whisper

print(chooser(true)('hey'))
